# Overview
#
# A vague attribution credits a claim to a source that the sentence
# never names (`Experts agree that the cache helps`). The reader cannot
# check the claim or weigh its source. The fix names the source or drops
# the claim. The rule sits at Recommended, because `many believe` also
# matches a question, and no sample measures the precision of the rule.
#
# Expression
#
# `main` lists the phrases. `claim` holds the verbs that count after
# `experts` and `critics`, and `finding` holds the verbs that count
# after `studies`, `research`, and `industry reports`. `many developers
# think`, `some say`, and `conventional wisdom` credit a claim to a
# crowd the same way.
#
# The `studies` and `research` branches carry the guard `!( !@tagged)`,
# and the guard skips a citation in parens or brackets (`Research shows
# (Smith 2021) that`). Harper's part-of-speech tagger marks each word
# with a tag for its word class, and `tagged` lists those tags. A paren
# or a bracket does not carry a tag, so `!@tagged` matches it, and the
# outer `!` then rejects the phrase. A word carries a tag, so the phrase
# still counts before a word. The tagger tags only words, so a guard on
# `PUNCT` (a punctuation mark) or on `SYM` (a symbol) does not match a
# paren or a bracket. A space in a pattern is a whitespace token, so the
# guard sits glued to the verb and reads the token right after the
# space.
#
# False Positives
#
# `research shows` and `studies have shown` pass when a citation in
# parens or brackets follows, because the sentence then names its
# source. `industry reports` needs a finding verb after it, because the
# phrase alone names real filings (`industry reports are filed
# monthly`).
#
# A citation at the end of the sentence still draws a finding (`Research
# shows that review catches bugs (Smith 2021)`), because the guard reads
# only the next token. `many believe` also matches a question (`how many
# believe it?`), and technical prose rarely asks one.
#
# Related Rules
#
# The Optional ProsePassive also reports the `it is` phrases and the `is
# widely` phrases of this rule, except a phrase with `known`. `It is
# widely believed that Go is fast` and `The format is widely regarded as
# stable` therefore each draw a finding from both rules, and no guard
# separates the two.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule did not hit any line in the measure.py corpus, so no sample
# measures its precision.
expr tagged [ADJ, ADP, ADV, AUX, CCONJ, DET, INTJ, NOUN, NUM, PART, PRON, PROPN, SCONJ, SYM, VERB, X]
expr claim [believe, say, agree, suggest, warn, recommend, argue, claim]
expr finding [show, shows, showed, suggest, suggests, indicate, indicates]
expr main [
    (experts @claim),
    (according to experts),
    (industry reports @finding),
    (some critics),
    (critics @claim),
    ([studies, research] @finding!( !@tagged)),
    ([studies, research] [has, have] shown!( !@tagged)),
    (many believe),
    (many [people, developers, engineers, users] [believe, think, say, agree]),
    (some say),
    (conventional wisdom),
    (it is [widely, generally, commonly, often] [believed, known, said, accepted]),
    (is widely [regarded, considered, seen])
]
let message "Vague attribution. Name the source or delete the claim."
let description "Flags a claim credited to unnamed experts, studies, or critics."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Research shows (Smith 2021) that review catches bugs."
allows "Industry reports are filed monthly to the regulator."
allows "The 2021 DORA report found that review catches bugs."
